## ABSTRACT
The development of thermodynamically controllable synthetic strategy to manipulate the morphology of ZIF-8 without capping agent is essential to help understanding their facet effect and the structure-activity relationship of single atom catalysts derived from ZIF-8. Here, we prepared ZIF-8 with different morphologies (cube, truncated rhombododecahedral and rhombododecahedral) and thus area ratio of exposed {100}, {110} facets by a thermodynamically controllable synthetic strategy. When the reaction proceeds under room temperature \((30\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\), the assembling of ZIF-8 followed an area-reducing layered growth mode, while switched to an integral layered growth mode at lower temperature \(-40\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Moreover, this strategy also works to obtain ZIF-8 encapsulated with metal precursors \((\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{acac})_{3}\), \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{acac})_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{acac})_{2}\)). Single Fe atom anchored on nitrogen doped carbon catalysts (SA-Fe/CN) derived from Fe-ZIF-8 retain their original morphologies and the unsaturated surface-active sites on {100} facet, which further displays different catalytic performance towards oxygen reduction reaction (ORR). This work not only reveals the different growth pattern of ZIF-8, but also points out a new direction for designing and synthesizing MOFs with different morphology rationally.
## KEYWORDS
ZIF-8, facet effect, single atom catalyst, oxygen reduction reaction
## 1 Introduction
Single atom catalysts (SACs) derived from metal organic frameworks (MOFs) have drawn tremendous attention [1–3], because the high specific surface area and unique micropores matrix of MOFs expose large number of active sites that endows SACs with high catalytic properties [4–7]. Moreover, single atomic sites in such catalysts also possess predictable and flexible coordination structure. On the one hand, metal atoms occupy the Zn sites by the evaporation of Zn or maintain the original structure in MOFs by etching the excess particles during the synthesis [8–12]. On the other hand, the addition of heteroatoms (S, P, Se, B etc.) [13–16] and the alteration of different MOFs [17–19] in the synthetic strategy can largely affect the coordinate sites or the carrier of SACs. Thus, such catalysts have been widely studied in many field [20–25], especially in the field of electrocatalysis [26–30], such as oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) [31–35], \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction reactions \((\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR})\) [36], formic acid oxidations (FAOR) [37] et al. For example, Yuan et al. [38] chose carboxylate/amide mixed-linker MOF (DMOF) and \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) as precursors to synthesize Co@DMOF-900, which shows 8-fold higher turnover frequency \((2.015\;\mathrm{e}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{site}^{-1})\) than Co SACs derived from ZIF-8; Jiang and coworkers [39] successfully synthesized \(\mathrm{Fe}_{1}/\mathrm{N}{-}\mathrm{C}\) by pyrolyzing PCN 222 with mixed ligand containing M-tetrakis((4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin) (M-TCPP; \(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{F}\mathbf{e}\), Co, Ni and \(\mathrm{Cu}\)) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\)-TCPP, which can effectively reduce \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) under a pressure as low as 0.15 atm.
