In recent years, several efforts have been employed to explore the morphology and facet effects of MOFs [40, 41]. The orientation of micropores and the coordination number of surface metal sites are highly dependent on the exposed facets, resulting in the different catalytic performance. For example, Datta et al. [42] reported that AlFFIVE-Ni exposed with different facets possesses different orientation of micro channels and nanosheets with maximally exposed {001} facets exhibit the highest selectivity of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in gas separation. Liu and his co-workers [43] devoted their efforts in the controllable synthesis of ZIF-8 with different morphology by using cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) as capping agent and found that Zn species in {100} facet have higher coordination number than that on {110} facet. Since single atomic sites usually maintain the original coordination structure of MOFs, the distinction of MOFs with various facets may also affect the electronic structure of finally formed active sites, which leads to the difference of catalytic properties of SACs. However, few works concentrated on the facet effect of MOFs derived SACs so far. Capping agents are always utilized in the
morphology controllable synthesis of MOFs, which coordinates to the surface sites and leads to the different coordination structure of surface atomic sites. Therefore, the development of the thermodynamically controllable synthetic strategy to obtain MOFs with specific exposed facets under a uniform reaction system is of great significance for understanding the growth mechanism of MOFs and the facet effect on SACs derived from MOFs.
ZIF-8 shows a typical truncated rhombic dodecahedral morphology in methanol or DMF solution, but it changes to cube when N-cetyl-N,N,N-trimethoxlammonium bromide is used as the capping agent [44, 45]. Herein, we successfully achieve the fabrication of ZIF-8 exposed with {100}, {110}, and mixed facet with different ratio by accurately controlled the synthesis temperature. Under \(30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), ZIF-8 grows along the {110} facet via an area-reducing layered growth mode to form truncated rhombic dodecahedral (ZIF-8-tr) and rhombic dodecahedral (ZIF-8-r) morphology successively. Surprisingly, when the synthetic system performs under a temperature as low as \(-40\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the morphology change will disappear and only ZIF-8 nanocubes (ZIF-8-c) can be obtained by an integral layered growth mode. Moreover, the two-growth process does not change in the presence of metal precursors \((\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{acac})_{3}\), \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{acac})_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{acac})_{2}\)). Via same calcination process, single metal atom anchored on nitrogen doped carbon catalysts (SA-M/CN) are obtained by pyrolyzing corresponding metal immobilized ZIF-8 (M/ZIF-8) with different morphology and exposed facet. Extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectra analysis reveals that the coordination number of Fe in SA-Fe-c is lower than that in SA-Fe-r and SA-Fe-tr. Besides, SA-Fe/CN-s also shows different ORR performance. The half-wave potential of SA-Fe/CN-c \((E_{1/2}=0.868\,\mathrm{~V~})\) is higher than both SA-Fe/CN-tr \((E_{1/2}=0.859\,\mathrm{~V~})\) and SA-Fe/CN-r \((E_{1/2}=0.851\,\mathrm{~V~})\), indicating that the unsaturated coordination of single atom Fe boosts the catalytic performance of ORR.
## 2 Results and discussion
To complete the facet controllable synthesis strategy, we firstly explored the growth process of ZIF-8. When 2-methylimidazole and zinc nitrate were sonicated at room temperature for a short time \((5\,\mathrm{min})\), the monodispersed ZIF-8-c with cubic morphology dominated by {100} facets can be observed in scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image (Fig. 1(a)). However, ZIF-8-tr exposed with both {100} and {110} facets was observed by continuing stirring for \(1\,\mathrm{~h~}\) (Fig. 1(b)). These results illustrate that: 1) It is possible to synthesize cubic ZIF-8 without capping agent; 2) the ZIF-8-c can be used as crystal seeds during the synthetic process; 3) this reaction system can be utilized as a perfect model to deeply recognize the thermodynamically growth process.
