urface and forms the hexagonal or rhombic with {110} facet. Interestingly, when the growth performed at \(-40\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with a similar size \((193\,\mathrm{nm})\) of nanocube seeds (Fig. 2(f)), no obvious morphology changes can be seen after a growth time of \(72\,\mathrm{h}\). With the increasing of reaction time, the average side length of square from nanocubes became longer and longer \((225\,\mathrm{nm}\) in \(12\,\mathrm{h}\), \(264\,\mathrm{nm}\) in \(24\,\mathrm{h}\), \(562\,\mathrm{nm}\) in \(72\,\mathrm{h}\)) and the
morphology maintained (Figs. 2(f)–2(i)), which indicate an integral layered growth at \(-40\,^{\circ}\!C\). During this process, the molecule cages not only fabricated on the \(\{100\}\) surface, but also on the edges and corners, meaning that the new formed layer can cover the integral surface of ZIF-8 to form a larger cube.
Unlike metal nanoparticles with the isotropic metallic bond [46], ZIF-8 was formed with directivity by the coordinate bonds between Zn ions and 2-methylimidazole. Under a low temperature \((-40\,^{\circ}\!C)\), the pendant group coordinated with 2-methylimidazole and Zn ions identically to maintain the cubic morphology and form a kinetic product of ZIF-8-c. The \(\{110\}\) facet with more saturated Zn sites decreases the surface energy and thus can be obtained via a higher temperature as the thermodynamic product.
To further explore the synthetic strategy of SACs derived from ZIF-8 with different morphology, we first tested the influence of metal precursors \((\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{acac})_{3}\), \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{acac})_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{acac})_{2}\)) on the synthetic method (M/ZIF-8). When the growth proceeds at \(-40\,^{\circ}\!C\), well-defined nanocubes are obtained (Figs. 3(a)–3(c)). M/ZIF-8 also shows a truncated rhombic dodecahedral morphology, when the reaction performed at \(30\,^{\circ}\!C\) for a short time (1 h, Figs. 3(d)–3(i)) and a rhombic dodecahedral morphology with extending the reaction time to \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) (Figs. 3(g)–3(i)). Moreover, the PXRD patterns of Fe/ZIF-8 with different morphology demonstrate that the encapsulated metal precursors do not change the crystallographic features and \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{acac})_{3}\) is uniformly distributed in ZIF-8 (Fig. S7 in the ESM). These results suggest that the addition of metal precursors have less effect on the morphology controllable synthetic strategy and thus can be used in the further synthesis of SACs with different morphology.
Single atom Fe anchored on nitrogen doped carbon catalysts (SA-Fe/CN) are reported as excellent catalysts toward ORR [47–49]. Thus, we selected ORR as a model reaction to evaluate the facet effect on SA-Fe/CN. At first, SA-Fe/CN with different morphology (namely SA-Fe/CNs, SA-Fe-c/CN, SA-Fe-tr/CN, SA-Fe-r/CN) were obtained by pyrolyzing Fe/ZIF-8 under \(900\,^{\circ}\!C\).
Only two broad peaks located at \(20^{\circ}{-}30^{\circ}\) and \(40^{\circ}{-}50^{\circ}\) respectively can be observed in the PYRD patterns [50], which is attributed to amorphous graphitic carbon (Fig. S8 in the ESM). SEM and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images exhibit that the three materials (SA-Fe/CNs) maintain their original morphology after pyrolysis (Figs. S9–S11 in the ESM and Figs. 4(a)–4(c)), and no obvious Fe particles or clusters could be detected. BET adsorption–desorption isotherms and porosity distributions (Fig. S12 and Table S2 in the ESM) show that SA-Fe-c/CN all retain high surface area and pore distributions of Fe/ZIF-8. From element dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) mapping, we noted that Fe, N and C elements were homogeneously distributed throughout the architecture (Figs. S13–S15 in the ESM). Single Fe atoms were observed by aberration corrected high angle annular dark field scanning TEM (AC-HAADF-STEM) analysis, and isolated bright spots attributed to single Fe atoms are marked with red circles (Figs. 4(d)–4(f)). C 1s and N 1s X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra [51] show no obvious differences, indicating the chemical states of C and N are similar in SA-Fe/CNs (Figs. S16 and S17 in the ESM). The Fe content in this catalyst was obtained based on coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy (ICPOES) and the results are displayed in Table S3 in the ESM.
